"45f_copper.mst — Copper bars: the oldest trick in the raster book, and the
 exact opposite of the direct framebuffer (docs/DIRECT_SCREEN.md). Launch it
 from the Demos menu, or a Workspace:

     Copper launch

 SPACE changes the colour scheme, Escape quits.

 THE POINT. The screen is filled ONCE, on the first frame, with palette index 1
 — every one of the 76,800 pixels. After that NO PIXEL IS EVER TOUCHED AGAIN.
 What moves is not the picture but the meaning of the colour: each frame
 rewrites what index 1 IS on each of the 240 scanlines, and three bars appear
 to ride up and down the screen.

 This is what the per-scanline palette is for, and nothing else in the world
 demonstrates it — Galaxigans uses it for one tractor beam, which shows the
 mechanism but not the idea. On real hardware this was the copper: a
 raster-locked coprocessor that rewrote colour registers between scanlines,
 giving machines with no spare memory bandwidth full-screen animation for
 almost nothing. It works here for the same reason: the pane is INDEXED, so a
 colour lookup happens per pixel in the shader, and changing the table changes
 every pixel that refers to it.

 Set beside Plasma (world/45d) the pair make the whole point about where cost
 lives. Plasma stores 76,800 bytes a frame and sends no commands. Copper stores
 nothing at all and sends 240 — one per scanline. Two extremes of the same
 engine, and both are cheap for completely different reasons.

 The honest cost, since this file is about cost: 240 `linePaletteAt:` commands
 a frame IS the technique's price through this API, and unlike the pixels there
 is no shared-memory path for the line palette yet. It is small — a palette
 poke carries five small integers, where a blit carries a frame — but it is not
 free, and if it ever needs to be, the answer is the same one the pixels got.

 Colours 2..15 are per-line too, so the same trick could drive fourteen
 independent bands; this demo uses one and varies its colour, which is what
 makes the bars read as bars rather than as a gradient. In a headless VM every
 game primitive silently fails, so loading this class is harmless."
"I am the copper-bars demo: three colour bars riding up and down a screen that
 is never redrawn. Launch me from the Demos menu, or a Workspace: Copper
 launch. I fill every pixel once with palette index 1 and then never touch a
 pixel again — each frame I only rewrite what index 1 MEANS on each of the 240
 scanlines, which is how home computers with no memory bandwidth to spare got
 full-screen animation. SPACE changes my colour scheme, Escape quits. I am the
 opposite extreme from Plasma, which stores every pixel every frame and sends
 no commands at all."
Object subclass: Copper [
    | pane t sineTable scheme prevSpace painted |
    <classVars: Current>

    Copper class >> launch [ ^self new start ]
    Copper class >> current [ ^Current ]

    "Screen height, and how many entries the sine table holds. A table rather
     than `sin` per scanline: this runs 240 times a frame, and the values
     repeat — the demo-scene answer, and it keeps the inner loop integer."
    Copper class >> height [ ^240 ]
    Copper class >> tableSize [ ^512 ]

    start [
        Current := self.
        pane := GamePane new.
        t := 0.
        scheme := 1.
        prevSpace := false.
        painted := false.
        self buildSineTable.
        pane onStep: [ :frame | self stepWith: frame ]; run.
        ^self
    ]

    buildSineTable [
        "A sine table scaled to the screen height, built once. Entry `i` is
         `sin(2*pi*i/512)` mapped to 0..height-1, so a bar's centre is a table
         lookup and an add."
        | n half |
        n := Copper tableSize.
        sineTable := Array new: n.
        half := (Copper height - 40) / 2.0.
        1 to: n do: [ :i |
            sineTable at: i put:
                (((i - 1) asDouble * 6.283185307179586 / n asDouble) sin
                    * half + half + 20.0) truncated ].
        ^self
    ]

    sineAt: i [ ^sineTable at: (i \\ Copper tableSize) + 1 ]

    "── the three bars ───────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    barCentre: which [
        "Where bar `which` sits this frame. Three different speeds and phases,
         so they cross rather than travel together."
        which = 1 ifTrue: [ ^self sineAt: t ].
        which = 2 ifTrue: [ ^self sineAt: (t * 2) + 170 ].
        ^self sineAt: (t * 3) + 340
    ]

    barHalfHeight [ ^14 ]

    "── the colour scheme ────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Each bar contributes a colour that is brightest at its centre and fades
     to nothing at its edge; the scanline's colour is the sum, clamped. That
     summing is why overlapping bars go white rather than simply occluding."

    schemeCount [ ^3 ]
    currentScheme [ ^scheme ]

    barColour: which [
        "The (r g b) a bar contributes at full strength, per scheme."
        | s |
        s := scheme.
        s = 1 ifTrue: [
            which = 1 ifTrue: [ ^Array with: 255 with: 60 with: 60 ].
            which = 2 ifTrue: [ ^Array with: 60 with: 255 with: 90 ].
            ^Array with: 80 with: 120 with: 255 ].
        s = 2 ifTrue: [
            which = 1 ifTrue: [ ^Array with: 255 with: 200 with: 40 ].
            which = 2 ifTrue: [ ^Array with: 255 with: 110 with: 20 ].
            ^Array with: 200 with: 40 with: 90 ].
        which = 1 ifTrue: [ ^Array with: 200 with: 200 with: 220 ].
        which = 2 ifTrue: [ ^Array with: 120 with: 140 with: 200 ].
        ^Array with: 60 with: 70 with: 130
    ]

    backdropAt: y [
        "A slow vertical gradient behind the bars, so the screen is never flat
         even where no bar is."
        | v |
        v := 12 + (y // 12).
        ^Array with: v with: v with: (v + 14)
    ]

    lineColour: y [
        "The colour index 1 should MEAN on scanline `y`: the backdrop plus each
         bar's contribution, faded by distance from that bar's centre."
        | rgb r g b d w c |
        rgb := self backdropAt: y.
        r := rgb at: 1. g := rgb at: 2. b := rgb at: 3.
        1 to: 3 do: [ :which |
            d := (y - (self barCentre: which)) abs.
            d < self barHalfHeight ifTrue: [
                "Linear falloff, squared for a rounder-looking core."
                w := self barHalfHeight - d.
                w := w * w // self barHalfHeight.
                c := self barColour: which.
                r := r + ((c at: 1) * w // self barHalfHeight).
                g := g + ((c at: 2) * w // self barHalfHeight).
                b := b + ((c at: 3) * w // self barHalfHeight) ] ].
        r > 255 ifTrue: [ r := 255 ].
        g > 255 ifTrue: [ g := 255 ].
        b > 255 ifTrue: [ b := 255 ].
        ^(r bitShift: 16) + (g bitShift: 8) + b
    ]

    "── the frame ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    stepWith: frame [
        "One frame: fill the screen ONCE, then only ever rewrite the line
         palette. `painted` is the whole demo in one flag — after the first
         frame sets it, no pixel is written again for the rest of the run."
        self readSpaceFrom: frame.
        painted ifFalse: [
            "Every pixel becomes per-line colour index 1, once and for all."
            pane cls: 1.
            painted := true.
            self drawHud ].
        "Rewrite what colour index 1 MEANS on each of the 240 scanlines -
         straight into the palette buffer (SM4). This used to be 240
         `linePaletteAt:` commands a frame, which was the last bulk state in
         this engine still travelling as commands; it is now 960 byte stores
         and none."
        0 to: Copper height - 1 do: [ :y |
            pane lineMemoryAt: y index: 1 put: (self lineColour: y) ].
        t := t + 3.
        pane present.
        ^self
    ]

    drawHud [
        "On the text plane, so it costs nothing and — more to the point — does
         NOT need redrawing when the bars move, because the bars are not
         pixels."
        pane textPut: 'COPPER BARS' at: 1 row: 0 color: GamePane white.
        pane textPut: 'the screen is filled once, then never redrawn'
             at: 1 row: 1 color: GamePane lightGrey.
        "53 cells wide, so this line has to fit in 52 from column 1 - the
         plane clips rather than wrapping, silently."
        pane textPut: '960 BYTES A FRAME  SPACE RECOLOURS  ESC QUITS'
             at: 1 row: 28 color: GamePane lightGrey.
        ^self
    ]

    readSpaceFrom: frame [
        | down |
        down := frame keyHeld: GamePane keyA.
        (down and: [ prevSpace not ]) ifTrue: [
            scheme := scheme + 1.
            scheme > self schemeCount ifTrue: [ scheme := 1 ] ].
        prevSpace := down.
        ^self
    ]
]
